## CRITICAL: Rules of Hooks violation

`UserPanel` calls `useState` and `useEffect` **after** an early `return`:

```jsx
function UserPanel({ id }) {
  if (!id) return null;          // <-- bails out before any hook runs
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);   // hook #1 — runs on some renders only
  useEffect(() => { ... }, [id]);           // hook #2 — runs on some renders only
  ...
}
```

When `id` is falsy, zero hooks run this render; when `id` is truthy, two hooks run. React keys hooks by call order per render — an inconsistent count between renders throws (`Rendered fewer hooks than expected…`) or, worse, silently attaches hook state to the wrong slot. This is the highest-severity finding; the component is unsafe to render as written.

**Fix direction:** move all hook calls above the first `return`, then branch inside the returned JSX:

```jsx
function UserPanel({ id }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  useEffect(() => {
    if (!id) return;            // guard inside the effect, not above the hooks
    fetchUser(id).then(setUser);
  }, [id]);
  if (!id) return null;         // early return now sits *after* every hook
  return <div>{user?.name}</div>;
}
```

## MEDIUM: Race condition / stale response on `id` change

Separate from the hook-order bug, the effect has a real async bug. If `id` changes quickly (A → B), the `fetchUser(A)` promise can resolve **after** `fetchUser(B)` and overwrite the newer result with the older user. The missing dependency here is not the issue — the dependency array is correct — the issue is that nothing cancels or ignores a superseded in-flight response.

**Fix direction:** guard against the stale response, either with an `ignore` flag or an `AbortController`:

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  if (!id) return;
  let ignore = false;
  fetchUser(id).then((u) => { if (!ignore) setUser(u); });
  return () => { ignore = true; };
}, [id]);
```

## LOW: `setUser` on a possibly-unmounted component

`fetchUser(id).then(setUser)` with no cleanup will call `setState` after unmount. In React 18 this no longer warns, but it's still wasted work and part of the same missing-cleanup shape as the race above — the `ignore` flag / `AbortController` cleanup fixes both at once.

---

